# The Winpthreads Library

This library provides POSIX threading APIs for mingw-w64.

## Supported Windows Versions

In the current state, `winpthreads` can be loaded on Windows NT 4.0 or later,
and on Windows 98 or later.

While `winpthreads` can be loaded on some Win9x systems, some parts of
the library are still not adopted to handle Win9x.

Support for older Windows NT versions and Win9x is in progress.

### Windows NT

You can define `WINPTHREADS_WINNT` macro to specify the minimal Windows NT
version you want `winpthreads` to be able to run on.

It can be defined to any `_WIN32_WINNT_*` macro from `sdkddkver.h` or their
corresponding values. For Windows NT 3.1, NT 3.5 and NT 3.51, which do not
have `_WIN32_WINNT_*` constans, you have to use appropriate values directly:

| Windows Version | Hex Value |
|-----------------|-----------|
| Windows NT 3.1  | `0x030A`  |
| Windows NT 3.5  | `0x0332`  |
| Windows NT 3.51 | `0x0333`  |

Default and allowed values for `WINPTHREADS_WINNT` are target-dependent:

| Target            | Minimal Allowed Value | Default Value                                           |
|-------------------|-----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| x86               | Windows NT 3.1        | Windows NT 3.1 with mingw-w64; Windows XP with MSVC     |
| x64               | Windows XP x64        | Windows XP x64                                          |
| arm32             | Windows 8             | Windows 8                                               |
| arm64 and arm64ec | Windows 10            | Windows 10                                              |

### Win9x

You can define `WINPTHREADS_WIN9X` macro to non-zero value in order to build
`winpthreads` with Win9x support.  
This macro is only meaningful for x86 and is ignored on other targets.

By default, it is enabled with mingw-w64 and disabled with MSVC.

## Linking Against Winpthreads

For maximum compatibility, winpthreads headers expose APIs without the
`dllimport` attribute by default.

If your program is linked against the DLL, you may define the macro
`WINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT` to add `dllimport` attributes to all APIs,
which makes function calls to them a bit more efficient.

Be careful when you build static libraries; you probably do not want to define
`WINPTHREADS_USE_DLLIMPORT` unconditionally, to ensure that your code can link
against either shared or static winpthreads. Note that winpthreads does not
export any variables, which means that `dllimport` is not requred, with both
MSVC and mingw-w64 toolchains.
